## Schedule

- **Date and time:** Monday, September 28, 2026, 12:00 UTC
- **Local times:** 20:00 HKT · 14:00 CEST · 08:00 EDT · 05:00 PDT

## Abstract

Quantum simulation is the most natural application of quantum computation, yet noisy quantum hardware still renders large-scale demonstrations challenging. In this talk, I present classical pre-processing routines that yield short-depth compilations for the two central building blocks of quantum simulation: initial state preparation and Trotter decomposition.

The former relies on a variational disentangling scheme for matrix product states (MPS), the latter on classical approximations of the Trotter error of parametrized circuits. Together, these routines enable optimized, short-depth circuit compilations that bring near-term quantum simulation demonstrations closer to reach.

## Speaker Bio

Refik Mansuroglu is a postdoctoral researcher in the group for Quantum Information and Quantum Many-Body Physics at the University of Vienna, supported by an ESPRIT Fellowship from the Austrian Science Fund (FWF). His research focuses on the intersection of quantum computing theory, classical simulation, and quantum complexity. He completed his PhD at the Friedrich-Alexander University of Erlangen–Nürnberg, during which he also spent time working at Los Alamos National Laboratory.
